Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) 

EDR is a a security solution that offers deep level protection for endpoints. It uses advanced algorithms to detect irregular behaviours.
The main features of an EDR are: 
1. Visibility
2. Detection
3. Response

EDR's detect and responds to threats automatically in real time.

An EDR also gives severity to alerts. It is the EDR that determines whether an alert is critical, high or low.

An antivirus can also detect some bascic threats but to detect advanced threats we need an EDR.
But how will an EDR protect against threats we have never seen before?

Let me use this analogy to explain.

Imagine an airport is an endpoint that needs protection. The first layer of protection is the immigration check. Let us liken
this to an antivirus. The immigration check monitors incoming people and matches their passports with a list of known criminals 
in its database. If there is a match, the entry is blocked. That seems like a good security protection.
But what happens if somebody who has no criminal history and is innocent looking tries to come in? The immigration check will let him in. 
Now, what if this innocent person is a thief that knows how to bypass basic security?

This is where an EDR comes in.
An EDR in this analogy would be the security officers stationed inside the airport. These security officers would constantly 
monitor the security cameras in the airport to see if there is anyone showcasing irregular behaviours. 
That is exactly how an EDR detects threats that are new and that have bypassed the basic antivirus security.

An example of an EDR tool is CrowdStrike Falcon while example of an antivirus is McAfee Endpoint Security. 

Note: No EDR tool will stop 100 percent of attacks but by capturing lots of security relevant information, it can help security
teams understand how attacks were successful and how to change their approach to ensure they are detected and blocked in the 
future.
